   "format": "mcq",
   "stem": {
      "text": "Let \\( f(x) = e^{2x} \\). The second-degree Taylor polynomial for \\( f \\) about \\( x = 0 \\) is used to approximate \\( f\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\). Find the Lagrange error bound for this approximation, taking \\( M \\) to be the maximum of \\( |f'''(x)| \\) on \\( \\left[0, \\frac{1}{2}\\right] \\)."
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": [
         "Divide",
         "ExponentialE",
         6
      ]
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "A degree 2 polynomial needs the derivative of order 3: \\( f'''(x) = 8 e^{2 x} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Multiply",
            8,
            [
               "Exp",
               [
                  "Multiply",
                  2,
                  "x"
               ]
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "Its absolute value is monotonic on \\( \\left[0, \\frac{1}{2}\\right] \\), so the maximum is at an endpoint: \\( M = 8 e \\). The bound is \\( \\frac{M\\,|x - 0|^{3}}{3!} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Multiply",
            8,
            "ExponentialE"
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "With \\( |x - 0| = \\frac{1}{2} \\), the bound is \\( \\frac{8 e \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{3}}{6} = \\frac{e}{6} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Divide",
            "ExponentialE",
            6
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "The error is at most \\( \\frac{e}{6} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Divide",
            "ExponentialE",
            6
         ]
      }
   ],
